WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:09.540
In this module, we're going to discuss UNIX processes along with program execution, pipes

2
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.980
and threads, and let's get started by asking what are the functions and interfaces for

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.780
ZOS UNIX system services.

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:23.300
The XPG 4.2 API is defined in a set of standards from the open group.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.940
This API defines some general operating system functions as well as some functions that

6
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.300
are specific to a UNIX system.

7
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.800
Functions which conform to XPG 4.2 UNIX are fully supported by the UNIX System Services

8
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.060
or USS API.

9
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.940
The XPG 4.2 functions are defined in terms of C language bindings.

10
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.900
When a ZOS UNIX program requests a system service, the following events take place.

11
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.980
First of all, the application programmer codes a C language statement with a function request,

12
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.020
for example, fork.

13
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.060
Fork requests that a new process be created.

14
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.140
During compilation of the program, a section of code called a stub is included in the compiled

15
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.460
module.

16
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:15.460
When the program is run, the stub identifies the runtime library or the RTL routine corresponding

17
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.020
to the fork function and passes control to this routine.

18
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.540
For some functions, the service can be performed by the library routine itself.

19
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.740
For others such as fork, the library routine includes a call macro instruction that selects

20
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.620
the appropriate callable service to request by name.

21
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:42.820
The callable service named BPX1FRK in turn calls the appropriate ZOS service function.

22
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:49.380
And finally, the ZOS UNIX kernel performs the requested service.

23
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:55.940
The XPG 4.2 kernel is a ZOS address space which is started at IPL time and initialized

24
00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.580
with system parameters in a sys1.palmlib member.

25
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:06.780
All files are allocated to the kernel and all IO operations result in calls to the kernel.

26
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:13.420
Interactive users of ZOS UNIX services can log on to TSOE and issue the command OMVS.

27
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:19.820
The OMVS command schedules a local process, which is FPR, which shares the TSO address

28
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.620
space.

29
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.700
Additional address spaces can be created by shell commands.

30
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:30.940
The workload manager, or WLM, is used to start these address spaces.

31
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:37.300
Users attached to ZOS through a TCPIP network can use the R login or telnet capability to

32
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.580
enter the ZOS system.

33
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:44.500
Their identity is checked and a shell address space is created for them.

34
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.180
Additional address spaces can be created for them by the workload manager for their shell

35
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.180
commands.

36
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:57.020
ZOS provides support to allow an installation to re-customize and re-initialize the ZOS USS

37
00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.620
environment without re-IPLing the system.

38
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:05.260
The FOMVS comma shutdown operator command, along with the FOMS comma restart operator

39
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:11.700
command, can be used to shutdown and re-initialize the USS environment.

40
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.420
A process is created to run a program and associated resources to the program.

41
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.140
In ZOS UNIX a process resides in an address space and that is the basic unit of work

42
00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.620
that runs within that address space.

43
00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:30.860
A process is created by another process or by a request for a ZOS UNIX service.

44
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:36.100
A process that creates a new process is called a parent process and the new process is called

45
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:37.300
a child process.

46
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.460
A child process can be created by the fork callable service.

47
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:47.640
A process is identified by a process ID or a PID and it is associated with its parent

48
00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.260
process by a parent process ID or PPID.

49
00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.580
There are different types of processes.

50
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.860
There's user processes which are associated with the user program or a shell user.

51
00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:04.240
Kernel processes perform system functions for the kernel and daemon processes perform

52
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.120
continuous or periodic system functions.

53
00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:13.440
To get information about ZOS UNIX processes the shell command PS can be used.

54
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.960
A ZOS UNIX user can use this command to display information about all the active processes.

55
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.300
A super user can display information about all the processes in the system by using

56
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.240
the command PS minus uppercase A. PS is a very useful command when finding processes

57
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.080
and handling them.

58
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:40.560
COMVS, ASID equals NNN or A equals NNN displays process information for the specified hexadecimal

59
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.680
address space ID or ASID.

60
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.640
If the specified ASID is not a ZOS UNIX system services address space an error message will

61
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.320
be issued.

62
00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:57.160
The operator can display the status of a particular OMVS process and its threads with the following

63
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:04.400
command D OMVS comma PID equals and then the process ID.

64
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.400
This shows information about each thread of a multi-threaded application.

65
00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.020
The thread ID would be needed to cancel an individual thread.

66
00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.440
There are several ways to stop a ZOS UNIX process.

67
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.460
One option is the operator modify command.

68
00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:28.020
The term option allows the signal interface routine to receive control before termination.

69
00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.700
The force option prevents the signal interface routine from receiving control before the

70
00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.500
process is terminated.

71
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:40.500
A special option shut down equals fork in it can be used to bring down the system cleanly.

72
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:49.140
Be aware it cancels all current BPX-AS address spaces created as a result of spawn or fork.

73
00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.340
The kill shell command can be used to cancel a process.

74
00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:57.000
A user can cancel their own processes and a system administrator or a super user has authority

75
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.060
to kill other users' processes.

76
00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.060
Term sends a SIGTERM signal and kill sends a SIGKILL signal.

77
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:10.700
And the ZOS cancel command can be used to cancel an address space that contains a ZOS

78
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.260
UNIX process.

79
00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:17.700
If the address space contains multiple processes, cancel causes all of them to terminate.

80
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.220
UNIX System Services Processes is new.

81
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:26.260
Type PS to access the Processes panel, which allows authorized users to display information

82
00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.740
about ZOS UNIX processes.

83
00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.700
You can then use the command C to cancel the process and D to display information about

84
00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.020
the process.

85
00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:41.180
Use PRE, short for prefix, or other filters to limit the rows displayed to some processes.

86
00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:46.140
Use ARR for Arrange to place interesting columns on the first panel.

87
00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.140
Or on Job Name to keep rows from jumping around.

88
00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.380
Next up, we're going to ask, what is a demon?
